Object Lifecycle & Dunder Methods
​The Life of a Machine (Four Events, Three Dunders)
​COMMISSIONING  
​Concept: Nameplate fitted, counters zeroed, ready for work. Runs once, automatically, the moment the object is made.  
​Method: __init__(self, ...)  
​IDENTIFICATION  
​Concept: Someone asks "which machine is this?" Without a nameplate you get a memory address — useless on a shop floor.  
​Method: __str__(self)  
​SERVICE  
​Concept: The ordinary working life: your own methods doing the job.  
​Examples: start(), make_part(), status()  
​DECOMMISSIONING  
​Concept: Struck off the register: coolant drained, power isolated, tools returned. Runs when the object is destroyed.  
​Method: __del__(self)  
​Code Implementationclass Lathe:
    def __init__(self, tag, max_rpm=2000):
        self.tag = tag
        self._max_rpm = max_rpm
        self.__rpm = 0
        self.__parts = 0
        print(f"{self.tag} commissioned, {max_rpm} rpm rated")

    def __str__(self):                   # The nameplate
        return f"Lathe {self.tag} | {self.__rpm} rpm | {self.__parts} parts"

    def __del__(self):                   # The disposal procedure
        print(f"{self.tag} decommissioned after {self.__parts} parts")


L1 = Lathe("L-01")       # L-01 commissioned, 2000 rpm rated
print(L1)               # Lathe L-01 | 0 rpm | 0 parts <- your nameplate
del L1                  # L-01 decommissioned after 0 parts
